{
{#- Model-family logo (unitysvc/unitysvc-data#60): the catalog row shows the
    model's own mark rather than the provider's — the model is what a customer
    recognises, and the provider is already named beside it. Resolved from the
    model id, so no per-model parameter is needed and the populate script
    cannot destroy it. A model the registry does not know resolves to null,
    which creates no document and leaves the provider logo in place, so the key
    needs no conditional. -#}
  "logo": {{ {"$logo_preset": offering_name} | tojson }},
  "name": "{{ offering_name }}",
  "display_name": "{{ display_name }}",
{#- 3-paragraph marketplace description (the validator requires >=2 paragraphs;
    the populate script emits a one-line brief). Teaser + detail + how-to.
    Chat models are reached through Cohere's OpenAI-compatible surface and speak
    both OpenAI and Anthropic dialects (see the llm_translator below); embedding
    and transcription models are non-chat, so their how-to omits the dialects. #}
{%- set is_chat = service_type == 'llm' and not is_transcription | default(false) and not is_image_embedding | default(false) %}
{%- set _base = description | default(display_name ~ ' — a ' ~ service_type ~ ' model served by ' ~ provider_display_name, true) %}
{%- set _dual = ' — callable in both OpenAI and Anthropic API styles through the UnitySVC gateway.' %}
{%- set _brief = (_base ~ _dual) if ((_base | length) + (_dual | length) < 195) else _base %}
{%- set _ctx = ' with a ' ~ details.context_length ~ '-token context window' if details.context_length else '' %}
{%- if is_chat %}
{%- set _detail = display_name ~ ' is served by ' ~ provider_display_name ~ ' over an OpenAI-compatible API' ~ _ctx ~ '. Requests are proxied through the UnitySVC gateway and billed per token.' %}
{%- set _howto = 'Reach it through the UnitySVC gateway with your own ' ~ provider_display_name ~ ' API key (the byok channel). See the code examples for both styles.' %}
{%- else %}
{%- set _detail = display_name ~ ' is served by ' ~ provider_display_name ~ _ctx ~ ' and proxied through the UnitySVC gateway, which routes each request to the upstream and bills per token.' %}
{%- set _howto = 'Reach it through the UnitySVC gateway with your own ' ~ provider_display_name ~ ' API key (the byok channel). See the code examples for setup with the OpenAI SDK, the Cohere SDK, and cURL.' %}
{%- endif %}
{#- Dedicated interoperability paragraph: the dual-dialect endpoint is the
    platform differentiator and what makes services interchangeable enough to
    sit behind the gateway's composition primitives. #}
{%- set _interop = 'One endpoint, either dialect — send an OpenAI Chat Completions or an Anthropic Messages request and the gateway auto-detects the format, translates it for the upstream, and replies in the style you sent. Because every model on UnitySVC speaks both dialects, they are interchangeable behind a single client: swap providers without touching your code, and combine them with the gateway composition primitives — group (/g/), broadcast (/b/), chain (/c/) and failover (/f/).' %}
{#- Rebase note: main gained the required `summary` field (unitysvc#1838)
    after this branch was cut. The teaser paragraph IS the summary, so the two
    intents compose rather than compete — `_brief` becomes `summary`, the rest
    stays in `description`, and the validator's >=2-paragraph rule still holds. #}
  "summary": {{ _brief[:200] | tojson }},
  "description": {{ (_detail ~ '\n\n' ~ _howto ~ '\n\n' ~ _interop) | tojson }},
  "service_type": "{{ service_type }}",
{#- Platform capability vocabulary (unitysvc docs/capabilities.yml) — what the
    caller GETS. The populator writes this into every param file; the map here
    is the fallback for a param file that predates it. -#}
{%- set _cap = "speech-to-text" if is_transcription else ({"embedding": "embed"}.get(service_type, "chat")) %}
  "capabilities": {{ capabilities | default([_cap]) | tojson }},
  "status": "{{ status | default('ready') }}",
  "currency": "USD",
{#- No `tags` key. unitysvc#1962 closes the tag vocabulary (docs/tags.yml) and
    ingest now REJECTS anything outside it, which retires all three values this
    template declared: `ai` restated service_type, `gateway` restated
    gateway_type (DERIVED so it cannot drift), and `byok` restated the
    enrollment channel the gateway actually routes on. That channel name
    survives as the key of upstream_access_config below -- the routing contract
    is untouched, only the tag value is gone.

    The one tag a seller may declare, `feature:func-call`, is deliberately NOT
    set here, and docs/tags.yml names this repo as the reason it had to exist:
    of the repos that knew something about tool support, cohere suppressed it
    "nothing at all". The gap is still open. Cohere's /v2/models does return a
    per-model `features` list, but neither Cohere's API reference nor the
    generated SDK type (`GetModelResponse.features`, documented only as "the
    features that the model supports") enumerates its values, so keying the tag
    off a token we have not observed would be a guess about the vocabulary; and
    this catalog mixes command, Aya, translate, vision, reasoning, embedding
    and transcription models, so it is not a catalog-wide fact either. Cohere's
    own tool-use docs publish no per-model support list to check it against.

    Blocked on an OBSERVED `features` payload from a live /v2/models call --
    which is also blocked on the unfixed pagination bug there (the unpaginated
    call returns 20 of 32 models, so an enumeration is not yet trustworthy).
    Until then these offerings stay untagged: under-claiming is recoverable,
    while a false tag ships a tool-calling example that answers
    `400 invalid request: tool use is not supported by the provided model`.

    Rendering no key at all is equivalent to an empty list here -- ingest's
    compose_service_tags replaces the seller-owned half from each upload -- so
    the legacy tags are cleared on the next upload rather than left behind. #}
{#- For chat models, merge the dual-dialect formats into the provider's details
    (R8); the llm_translator below exposes the OpenAI-compatible upstream in both
    dialects. Non-chat (embedding/transcription) models keep details as-is. #}
{%- if is_chat %}
{%- set _details = details.copy() %}
{%- set _ = _details.update({"input_formats": ["openai", "anthropic"], "output_formats": ["openai_response", "anthropic_response"]}) %}
  "details": {{ _details | tojson }},
{%- else %}
  "details": {{ details | tojson }},
{%- endif %}
{%- if payout_price %}
  "payout_price": {{ payout_price | tojson }},
{%- endif %}
  "upstream_access_config": {
    "byok": {
      "access_method": "http",
      "base_url": "{{ api_base_url }}",
      "api_key": "${ customer_secrets.{{ env_api_key_name }} }",
      "rate_limits": [],
      "routing_key": {"model": "{{ offering_name }}"}
{#- The Anthropic SDK hardcodes /v1/messages, which the translator maps to the
    OpenAI chat endpoint. Cohere serves that off the standard path (its
    OpenAI-compatible surface lives under /compatibility/v1), so point the
    translator at the real upstream path -- the OpenAI-SDK and Cohere-SDK
    surfaces reach their own paths via the customer URL and are unaffected. #}
{%- if is_chat %},
      "request_transformer": {
        "llm_translator": {
          "direction": "anthropic_to_openai",
          "upstream_path": "/compatibility/v1/chat/completions"
        }
      }
{%- endif %}
    }
  }
}
